SED(1)                        User Commands

NAME
    sed - stream editor

SYNOPSIS
    sed [-Ealnru] command [file...]
    sed [-Ealnru] [-e command] [-f command_file] [-i extension] [file...]

DESCRIPTION
    Reads the files (or standard input) line by line, applies the editing
    commands to every line and writes the result to standard output. This
    is the sed of FreeBSD. -n prints only lines selected with p; -E uses
    extended regular expressions; -i extension edits the files in place and
    keeps a copy under the old name plus the extension (-i '' keeps none);
    -a delays opening w files; -l makes output line buffered; -r is -E;
    -u is unbuffered. Several -e and -f options are concatenated.

    A command has an optional address (a line number, $, /regex/, or two of
    them separated by a comma) followed by a function letter: s/re/rep/flags
    (flags g, p, N, I, w file), y/from/to/, p, d, D, n, N, g, G, h, H, x, q,
    =, l, a, i, c (text on the following line after a backslash), r file,
    w file, b label, t label, T label, :label, ! (negation) and { } groups.
    A label extends to the end of the line, so branch commands are given
    with separate -e options or on their own lines.

SEE ALSO
    awk(1), grep(1), index(7)
